[{:db/id 17592186119945 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-eb19afc5-d677-5f22-bac0-92428e650b61"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-eb19afc5-d677-5f22-bac0-92428e650b61" :question/id #uuid "eb19afc5-d677-5f22-bac0-92428e650b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-126900" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t,4t-1⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =⟨-c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=-c \\\\ 4=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain that $c=4.$ Substituting $c=4$ into the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}-2(-1),4(-1)-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(3,-5).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-236f2410-bb87-524a-b10a-c030da587949"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-236f2410-bb87-524a-b10a-c030da587949" :answer-field/id #uuid "236f2410-bb87-524a-b10a-c030da587949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-38f7dbd3-9ac6-59ba-822d-baee2e1a48d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4ad7606a-78cf-5b5a-aad3-516d0783136b" "answer-b2e66482-ad5e-56f0